  "agent": "The Secretary of Defense",
  "agent_role": "decide",
  "situation": "The Department of Defense is considering where to place a new Defense Intelligence Agency. The options under consideration include placement in the Office of the Secretary of Defense or under the Joint Chiefs of Staff, among other arrangements. One concern is how to integrate intelligence with strategic planning and military operations; another is whether Joint Chiefs management could weaken department-wide direction or access. Proponents of placement in the Office of the Secretary of Defense emphasize management decisions and civilian control, while opponents emphasize integration with operational planning. A proposed charter could specify safeguards concerning the Director's rank, powers, functions, and access.",
  "question": "What should the Secretary of Defense do about the Defense Intelligence Agency's organizational placement?",
  "facts": [
    {
      "id": "F1",
      "kind": "situation",
      "status": "confirmed",
      "fact": "The organizational alternatives considered included placement under the Joint Chiefs of Staff, in the Office of the Secretary of Defense, under a military department, or directly under the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff.",
      "spans": [
        "src:8823919736b1#p1.55",
        "src:8823919736b1#p1.56",
        "src:8823919736b1#p1.59",
        "src:8823919736b1#p1.67",
        "src:8823919736b1#p1.78"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "F2",
      "kind": "authority",
      "status": "confirmed",
      "fact": "The National Security Act, as amended, states that the Joint Staff shall have no executive authority, which the proposal identifies as barring assignment of the agency under the Joint Staff.",
      "spans": [
        "src:8823919736b1#p1.57"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "F3",
      "kind": "situation",
      "status": "assessed",
      "fact": "The assessment was that assigning the agency to one military department would complicate existing relationships because defense intelligence spans the departments and is connected to strategic planning and unified-command operations.",
      "spans": [
        "src:8823919736b1#p1.58"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "F4",
      "kind": "capability",
      "status": "assessed",
      "fact": "Advocates of placement in the Office of the Secretary of Defense argued that an Assistant Secretary, an Assistant to the Secretary, or an agency head reporting directly to the Secretary could make resource and management decisions and support civilian control.",
      "spans": [
        "src:8823919736b1#p1.60",
        "src:8823919736b1#p1.64"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "F5",
      "kind": "situation",
      "status": "assessed",
      "fact": "Opponents of placement in the Office of the Secretary of Defense argued that separating intelligence staff responsibility from operational staff responsibility would impede integration of intelligence with strategic planning and direction.",
